--TEST--
A settings object may ask for a weaker `tls` and is refused rather than quietly given the stronger one, and the SQLite arm is a shape the checker accepts and this build cannot open — `rule:core-classes/db-capabilities`, `rule:core-api/shape-arms-are-disjoint`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: Core\Db::Tls
// `rule:core-classes/db-capabilities`'s third closed hole. PHP's `pdo_pgsql` defaults to
// `sslmode=prefer` and connects in plaintext whenever the server says so; here
// the weak spelling exists, is writable, and is answered by a refusal that
// names what it asked for. Verifying more than a caller asked for would be a
// promise made quietly, which is the failure mode this is the opposite of.
try {
    var $plain = Core\Db::open({
        driver: Core\Db\Driver::Postgres,
        host: "db.example.test",
        database: "shop",
        user: "app",
        password: "hunter2",
        tls: Core\Db::Tls::Disabled,
    });
    echo "opened without TLS", "\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $refused) {
    echo $refused->message, "\n";
}

// The refusal above landed before the `db.open` grant was consulted, and this
// deployment grants nothing at all -- so the ordering is the assertion: what a
// settings object *says* is judged before what a deployment allows, exactly as
// `Core\Db::connect` judges its options before it consults the grant.

// The other arm of `Db\Settings`, which takes a `path` and declares no `host`
// at all. It type-checks, which is what makes it an arm, and what refuses it
// here is this deployment's file rule: `rule:core-classes/db-capabilities`
// asks `fs.read` about the path on this arm, there being no host to ask
// `db.open` about.
try {
    var $file = Core\Db::open({
        driver: Core\Db\Driver::Sqlite,
        path: "/srv/shop/shop.db",
    });
    echo "opened a file", "\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $refused) {
    echo $refused->message, "\n";
}

echo "still running", "\n";
--FILE nvs.toml--
# A CLI run reads every file unless `read` is written.
[capabilities.fs]
read = []
--EXPECTF--
Core\Db::open: `tls` is `Tls::Disabled`, but every TCP connection uses `Tls::VerifyFull`. A weaker mode is not allowed. For a private certificate authority, set `tls_ca_file` in the `[db.<name>]` block. The certificates are then still checked
Core\Db::open needs the capability `fs.read` for %sshop.db, which is not granted
help: add `read = ["%s"]` under `[capabilities.fs]` in nvs.toml
still running
